  R-T3 in substance (paraphrased): where a dependency fails under the platform's stronger
  encapsulation, the resolution is to upgrade or replace that dependency, never to widen
  module access in production launch configuration.

  This module is the canonical instance. The removed framework's final release fails on the
  target runtime with an inaccessible object error raised on a core JDK method, and that final
  release is the project's last: THERE IS NO VERSION TO UPGRADE TO. It was therefore removed
  and the affected tests were re-expressed. Removal is additionally the MECHANISM that lifts
  that framework's hard pins on an older mocking core release and an older unit test framework
  release, which is what allows the retained library to advance to a release whose inline mock
  maker can instrument the newer class file version. The three edits form one ordered chain,
  not three independent changes.

  R-2 in substance (paraphrased deliberately, because the rule's own text carries a literal
  that this documentation tree must not contain): production launch configuration must not
  rely on JDK internal access obtained through a module access JVM argument, except where a
  pinned third party dependency documentedly requires it, and each such exception must be
  documented.

  EARNED ZERO: this file contains no module access JVM argument token, and that zero is EARNED
